 In to Prop Up Ken Paxton in Texas=0AThe money in the Senate race in Texas =
dwarfs every other contest in the =0Acountry, with combined spending fast a=
pproaching a quarter-billion dollars. A =0Ayear ago, few would have imagine=
d a state that hasn=E2=80=99t elected a Democrat to =0Astatewide office sin=
ce 1994 would host the most expensive and fiercely =0Acontested Senate race=
 in the country. But the combination of a scandal-scarred =0AGOP nominee in=
 Ken Paxton and a terrible midterm environment for Republicans =0Ahas given=
 Democrat James Talarico a shot at a historic upset, if the tsunami of =0AR=
epublican money ($193 million and counting) doesn=E2=80=99t overwhelm him.=
=0A=0AIt=E2=80=99s a measure of just how dire things are for Republicans th=
at the four most =0Aexpensive Senate races after Texas =E2=80=94 Ohio, Mich=
igan, Iowa and North Carolina =E2=80=94 =0Aare all states Trump won in 2024=
, where Republicans and their allies are =0Aoutspending Democrats. And yet =
polls show the Democratic nominees running =0Acompetitively or outright lea=
ding the races. In fact, Democrat Roy Cooper=E2=80=99s =0Adouble-digit lead=
 in North Carolina just prompted the main Republican super =0APAC, the Sena=
te Leadership Fund, to effectively concede the state despite =0Ahaving spen=
t $30 million there. Itredirected its money to Kansas =0A<https://www.nytim=
es.com/2026/10/02/us/midterms-election-republicans-north-carolina-kansas.ht=
ml>=0A to prop up the flagging GOP incumbent, Senator Roger Marshall, who a=
 recent =0ANew York Times/Siena University poll showsis running dead even =
