margin-top:24px;font-size:16px">House races are much cheaper than Senate ra=
ces. But that=E2=80=99s small consolation for Republicans, because they hav=
e many more of them to defend. Once again, the money tells a clear story: T=
he 22 most expensive House races are all districts Trump won two years ago.=
 In six of those, combined spending has already eclipsed $10 million.</p><p=
 style=3D"margin-top:24px;font-size:16px">At the top of the list is Colorad=
o=E2=80=99s 8th District race between freshman Republican incumbent Gabe Ev=
ans and former state Representative Manny Rutinel. It=E2=80=99s a template =
of what=E2=80=99s happening in many districts across the country. In 2024, =
Evans rode the Republican wave, eking out a tight victory. But with the tid=
e turning, Democrats view his seat and others Republicans won narrowly two =
years ago as prime pickup opportunities in November.</p><p style=3D"margin-=
top:24px;font-size:16px">Other $10 million districts are money sinks becaus=
e the lines were redrawn to give one party or the other an advantage. In Ca=
lifornia, Democrats altered the 22nd District to try to oust Republican inc=
umbent David Valadao, who=E2=80=99s repeatedly survived past elections. As =
a measure of their commitment, they=E2=80=99ve outspent Republicans by more=
 than $1 million to make this the second-most expensive House race in the c=
ountry.</p><p style=3D"margin-top:24px;font-size:16px">The situation is rev=
ersed in south Texas, where the GOP redrew the 34th District with the aim o=
f defeating Democratic Representative Vicente Gonzalez. The new district fa=
vored Trump by roughly 10 percentage points in 2024. But public sentiment h=
as shifted so sharply against Republicans that the nonpartisan <em style=3D=
"font-size:16px">Cook Political Report</em> rates the race as a =E2=80=9Cto=
ss-up.=E2=80=9D Here again, Democrats smell blood: They=E2=80=99ve outspent=
 Republicans in hopes of keeping Gonzalez in Congress.</p><p style=3D"margi=
n-top:24px;font-size:16px">As with polling, the financial map can shift qui=
ckly, especially in the month before the election, as parties are forced to=
 make tough decisions about where to concentrate their spending and where t=
o cut bait. Republicans may have backed off North Carolina=E2=80=99s Senate=
 race, but my reading of Pyers=E2=80=99 figures shows them still spending s=
ignificant sums in at least six House districts that Kamala Harris won and =
that are either open seats or have Democratic incumbents.</p><p style=3D"ma=
rgin-top:24px;font-size:16px">As always, political junkies will be closely =
